                                    style: { "--framer-text-alignment": `justify` },
                                    children: `Second, I find that the decision of the "items" — now portrayed as cute creatures — that holds the abilities being required to be picked up and placed into a specific spot to be questionable. The flow of the interaction from picking up, to placing, to using the ability, feels unnecessarily intricate. This could  very well be simplified by making it as a "consumable", where it gives the player one charge of the ability. Even though "consuming" the creatures can have a sinister connotation — which contradicts the art direction of the game — I think there are still many ways to go around it. More on the needless intricacies, it would simplify the tutorial by a lot, considering that players have shown to have trouble learning the fundamental mechanics of picking, placing, and using the abilities during the playtests.`,
